# station-watch.service — RETIRED 2026-09-01. DO NOT ENABLE.
#
# THE POLICY THIS FILE VIOLATES
#   Nothing may poll or touch the Sukabumi station while there is no active
#   Claude session. Tom's decision, 2026-09-01: no monitoring and no station
#   interaction unattended. The watcher probes tcp/22, queries Grafana, and on
#   a wake opens an SSH session and pulls files. None of that may happen with
#   nobody watching it happen.
#
#   This unit was written for the opposite premise — that "a recovery that
#   lands while nobody is logged in is the whole case for the watcher" — and it
#   pursued that premise two ways, both now removed:
#     * `WantedBy=default.target` started it at every login, session or not.
#     * The INSTALL block told the reader to `sudo loginctl enable-linger`,
#       which would have kept it running past logout entirely.
#   It was `enable`d and running on 2026-09-01 and has been stopped and
#   disabled. Linger was never enabled and must stay off.
#
# HOW THE WATCH RUNS NOW
#   As a session-scoped process, so its lifetime is the session's:
#
#       cd .../liveorc_server/station-health
#       python3 -u ./pounce.py 2>&1 | tee -a \
#           ~/code/git/openrivercam/data/station-forensics/station-watch.log
#
#   The tee keeps the one continuous log this watch has always written; that
#   file's continuity across every arming is the record of what the station did,
#   and a fresh stream per arming would break it. Only ONE pounce may race for a
#   wake window, so check `pgrep -f pounce.py` before starting another.
#
#   The cost of this policy is real and accepted: wakes that land between
#   sessions are missed. The station is a pilot, and an unobserved recovery is
#   cheaper than an unobserved process holding SSH credentials against it.
#
# WHY THE FILE IS KEPT
#   As the record of the decision. A deleted unit gets rewritten by whoever next
#   notices the watcher dies with the terminal — which is how it got written the
#   first time. If that reasoning ever wins again, it has to answer this header.
